verdict
The bug is the predicate at source/priority-queue.ts line 93: `this.#findIndex(element => element.id !== id)` should match `element.id === id` to find the target. Dequeue/compaction mechanics are sound for id matching because dequeues only advance `#head` and null consumed slots, and `#findIndex` starts at `#head`; `#compact` restores the invariant before binary insertion. The public `PQueue.setPriority` wrapper is not the source of this bug, since it first locates the waiting task by strict id equality in `#waitingTasks` and only then delegates; the broken matching is in the default queue class.

reasoning
The fix is a one-predicate change: make `#findIndex` select the live element whose `id` equals the passed id. The surrounding logic (throw on `-1`, same-priority early return at `this.#queue[index]`, splice-and-re-enqueue via `enqueue(item.run, {priority, id})`) is already built around `#head`-relative live-range matching, so no structural change is needed. The wrapper's finite-priority validation stays in PQueue, but the direct PriorityQueue's unknown-id behavior becomes correct once the predicate matches.

test_plan
Run the focused default queue tests first: `node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-name-pattern='PriorityQueue' test/priority-queue.ts`. Expected: `PriorityQueue ignores consumed items with no live match`, `PriorityQueue setPriority works when only one live item remains`, `PriorityQueue setPriority with the current priority keeps the position...`, and `PriorityQueue setPriority with the current priority still throws for an unknown id` all pass. Then run full queue tests: `node --import=tsx/esm --test --test-timeout=60000 test/priority-queue.ts test/validation.ts test/debug.ts test/basic.ts test/advanced.ts`. Expected: validation unknown-id/TypeError cases, runningTasks cases, and the basic/advanced ordering assertions all pass. Both files currently report no TypeScript diagnostics (`No diagnostics for 'source/priority-queue.ts'` and `source/index.ts`), and `xo` should stay clean.

leftover
No environment failures. I did not execute the test suite (tooling here has no shell command execution), so the pass/fail predictions are from reading the predicate and assertions, not from observed test output. Tests already appear written to catch this exact inversion, but whether someone intended `!==` for any other reason is not resolved beyond it contradicting the test expectations.

